# What CI downloads, checks and installs before and during the gate (ADR-0017): CI runs the pull request's own copy of these files, so a change here decides what CI fetches and runs, and how it is checked. Every file in these two folders is such a file, and a new one is covered as soon as it is added.
# deps/ holds the pins. ubuntu-packages.lock names the base image of the Linux CI jobs and lists every Ubuntu package file that they install, with its size and SHA-256, taken from the package index that apt checked against Ubuntu's signature: those hashes are the only link between the files and that signature. qt.json names the Qt release, its date and the archives to install, which carry no hashes in this repository, so the installer checks them against those that download.qt.io publishes; bayan-core.json names the bayan-core commit whose C SDK CI builds, and with it the Rust version and the crates that the commit pins; requirements-tools.txt pins CMake, Ninja, clang-format and clang-tidy with their SHA-256.
/deps/ @Gabrielc1925
# scripts/ holds the programs that act on the pins. ubuntu-packages.sh fetches the Ubuntu package files, checks them against the lock and installs them with dpkg, which runs the packages' maintainer scripts as root in the CI containers. check-qt-release-age.py stops every job that installs Qt, before it does, unless the pinned release is at least 24 hours old; install-qt.py downloads Qt's archives, checks each against its published SHA-256 and every entry for unsafe paths and links, and extracts them; dev-setup.sh installs the pinned tools with pip --require-hashes and Qt with install-qt.py. build-engine-sdk.sh fetches the pinned bayan-core commit, checks that it is on bayan-core's main, installs that commit's Rust version with rustup and builds the C SDK; test-engine-sdk.sh builds the app against that SDK, or against a stand-in built from the stub engine, and runs the tests that use the engine.
/scripts/ @Gabrielc1925
# The verification gate's definition, code and configuration (cmake --workflow --preset verify, and CI's other presets): CI runs a pull request's own copy of them, so a change here can weaken the checks that judge it. CMakePresets.json defines the gate and CI's builds: warnings as errors, the lint, the sanitizers' settings and which tests run against the real engine. CMakeLists.txt switches the Qt licensing check, the compiler settings and the lint on for the whole project, and chooses which files and test programs the lint covers; tests/CMakeLists.txt registers every test of the gate, among them the supply-chain and licensing checks, and decides which of them may skip instead of fail. cmake/BayanQtPolicy.cmake is the Qt licensing policy: only reviewed LGPL modules, linked dynamically, and never the commercial qmlsc (ADR-0013); cmake/BayanCompilerOptions.cmake sets the warnings that fail the build, the hardening of the app's binaries and the sanitizers; cmake/BayanLint.cmake runs clang-format and clang-tidy, checks that they are exactly the versions in deps/requirements-tools.txt, and fails on any finding. .clang-tidy and tests/.clang-tidy choose clang-tidy's checks, among them bugprone, cert and the Clang static analyzer; .clang-format sets the format that the gate enforces; tests/lsan-suppressions.txt lists the leaks inside third-party libraries that LeakSanitizer may ignore.
